<% content_for :title, 'Campaign Settings' %>

<%= render 'help/page', title: 'Campaign Settings' do %>
  <div class='space-y-8'>

    <p class='text-fg'>
      Campaign settings govern the fundamental operating parameters of a campaign — its designation,
      operational type, and data acquisition protocols. These parameters determine how sectors are
      populated and what capabilities are available to the crew.
    </p>

    <div>
      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Name</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            The campaign's operational designation. Displayed throughout the interface and used to
            identify the campaign in mission briefings and reports.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Campaign Type</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Determines the campaign's operational context and how sectors are initialised.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Charted Space</span>
              — The standard operational mode. Sectors are imported from TravellerMap and the crew
              explores the known reaches of Charted Space. Build configurations are assigned
              manually or pulled from TravellerMap data.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Deepnight Revelation</span>
              — Configured for the Deepnight Revelation campaign. On creation, all Deepnight
              Revelation sectors are pre-populated with build configurations derived from the
              sourcebook. Survey index tracking is enabled by default.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Homebrew</span>
              — The universe is constructed from scratch. No sectors are imported automatically.
              All sectors, subsectors, and build configurations are defined by the referee.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Sector Data Source</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Visible only for Deepnight Revelation campaigns. Controls where build configurations
            are sourced when populating sectors.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>TravellerMap</span>
              — Build configurations are imported from TravellerMap data. Use this when you want
              sector data that reflects the published universe as catalogued by TravellerMap.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Deepnight Books</span>
              — Build configurations are sourced from the Deepnight Revelation sourcebook. Use
              this for a faithful rendering of the sectors as described in the published
              adventure materials.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Exploration Mode</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Marks the campaign as exploration-driven rather than charted space. Two effects follow:
            the jump log form gains a <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Survey Index</span> field,
            and newly created sectors default their build specification to
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>surveyIndex: 3</span> (lightly surveyed) instead of
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>surveyIndex: 10</span> (fully settled).
          </dd>
          <div class='mt-3'>
            <p class=' text-fg'>
              On saving a jump log, the survey index value is written to the destination star system —
              or to the destination parsec directly if no system exists at that location. The default
              value when logging a jump is <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>10</span>. Valid
              range is <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>0–12</span>, corresponding to the survey
              index scale used in the Deepnight Revelation sourcebook. This setting is enabled
              automatically for Deepnight Revelation campaigns, but can be toggled independently for
              any campaign type — a homebrew frontier-exploration campaign should enable it too.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>System Generation</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Sophont Check</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Controls the procedure used when determining whether a world has a native sophont species.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Standard</span>
              — Default sophont check procedure.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Rare Earth Universe Variant</span>
              — Applies the Rare Earth variant (<span class='italic'>World Builder's Handbook</span>, p.130).
              Applies a DM −2 to biocomplexity rolls. Significantly reduces the probability of complex life.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Very Rare Earth Variant</span>
              — House rule — not a standard Traveller rule. Applies a DM −3 to biocomplexity rolls,
              more severe than the Rare Earth variant (DM −2). Severely reduces the probability of
              complex life.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>None</span>
              — Sophont check is skipped entirely. No sophont worlds will be generated. This is the
              default for Charted Space campaigns.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Max Tech Level</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Sets a campaign-wide ceiling on the tech level assigned to any generated world. The
            generator will not produce worlds with a tech level above this value. The default is
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>16</span>, corresponding to the standard upper
            bound in Traveller. Valid range is <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>0–33</span>.
          </dd>
          <div class='mt-3'>
            <p class='text-fg'>
              Individual build configurations may specify their own
              <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>maxTechLevel</span>, which takes precedence
              over this campaign setting for that subsector generation run.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Use Native Sophont Tech Level Rules</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            When enabled, the tech level of worlds with a native sophont population is determined
            using native sophont rules rather than the standard procedure.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Standard rules (default)</span>
              — Tech level is generated using the standard Traveller procedure.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Native sophont rules</span>
              — Tech level is rolled as <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>3D3 − 2 + DMs</span>,
              where the DMs are determined as follows:
              <table class='mt-2 text-fg-muted border-collapse'>
                <thead>
                  <tr class='text-left text-fg'>
                    <th class='pr-4 pb-1 font-semibold'>Condition</th>
                    <th class='pb-1 font-semibold'>DM</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class='align-top'>
                  <tr><td class='pr-4 py-0.5'>Star age &lt; 1</td><td>−1</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class='pr-4 py-0.5'>Star age 2–3</td><td>+1</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class='pr-4 py-0.5'>Star age ≥ 4</td><td>+2</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class='pr-4 py-0.5'>Government code 1, 5, or 7</td><td>+1</td></tr>
                  <tr><td class='pr-4 py-0.5'>Population code &gt; 7</td><td>+1</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Allow Captive Government</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            When disabled, the generator will not assign government code 6 (Captive Government) to
            any world during subsector generation. A captive government implies the system is
            politically controlled by another system — in exploration campaigns this relationship
            should be a referee decision rather than a generated result.
          </dd>
          <div class='mt-3 space-y-2'>
            <p class=' text-fg'>
              Defaults to <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>disabled</span> for Deepnight
              Revelation campaigns and <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>enabled</span> for
              Charted Space and Homebrew campaigns.
            </p>
            <p class=' text-fg'>
              Individual build configurations may specify
              <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>allowCaptiveGovernment</span>, which takes
              precedence over this campaign setting for that subsector generation run.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Limit Populated World Eccentricity</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            When enabled, restricts every generated world with a population code of 3 or greater to
            the three lowest rolls of the Eccentricity table (<cite>World Builder's Handbook, pg. 27</cite>),
            i.e. an eccentricity of 0.09 or less — unless that world already has an explicit
            eccentricity value set, which always wins.
          </dd>
          <div class='mt-3 space-y-2'>
            <p class=' text-fg'>
              Defaults to <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>enabled</span> for Charted Space
              campaigns and <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>disabled</span> for Deepnight
              Revelation and Homebrew campaigns.
            </p>
            <p class=' text-fg'>
              Individual build configurations may specify
              <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>limitedMainWorldEccentricity</span>, which takes
              precedence over this campaign setting for that generation run.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Display Preferences</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Distance Display</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Controls which distance measurement is shown in star system tables and orbital data
            sections throughout the interface.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>AU (default)</span>
              — Distance from the primary star is displayed in Astronomical Units.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Orbit #</span>
              — Distance is displayed as the Traveller orbit number (0–19 scale).
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Date Format</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Controls how Imperial dates are displayed throughout the interface.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Traveller Standard (default)</span>
              — Dates are displayed in day-year format, e.g. 001-1105.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>ISO 8601</span>
              — Dates are displayed in year-day format, e.g. 1105-001.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Map</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Sector Capital Colour</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Colours the name label of any star system whose main world carries the
            <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Cs</span> (Sector Capital) trade code, on the
            subsector map, sector map, poster, and interactive starmap. Leave blank to use the
            default name colour. When set, a matching legend entry appears on the poster.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Subsector Capital Colour</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Colours the name label of any star system whose main world carries the
            <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Cp</span> (Subsector Capital) trade code, the
            same way as Sector Capital Colour above. If a world carries both trade codes, the
            Sector Capital Colour takes precedence.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Hex Size</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Controls the pixel size of hexes on the subsector map, sector map, poster, and
            in-app viewport maps rendered by Rails.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Small</span>
              — Compact hexes (30px), fitting more of the map on screen.
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Medium (default)</span>
              — Standard hex size (40px).
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Large</span>
              — Larger hexes (50px), making icons and labels easier to read.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>API Access</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Campaign Slug</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            The campaign's unique identifier as it appears in all URLs — the
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>:campaign_slug</span> segment of
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>/c/:campaign_slug/…</span>. Enter this value
            in the Foundry module's <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>MTU Campaign Slug</span>
            setting.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>API Token</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            A shared secret used to authorise write operations from external tools such as the
            Foundry VTT module. The token is transmitted as a
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Bearer</span> credential in the
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Authorization</span> header of each request.
            Enter this value in the Foundry module's
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>MTU API Token</span> setting.
          </dd>
          <div class='mt-3 space-y-2'>
            <p class=' text-fg'>
              The token is generated automatically when a campaign is created and is not
              transmitted or stored anywhere other than this page. Use the
              <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Copy</span> button to transfer it to the
              Foundry module settings.
            </p>
            <p class=' text-fg'>
              Use <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Regenerate</span> to issue a new token —
              for example if the token is compromised. The previous token is invalidated immediately.
              Any external tool using the old token will receive a
              <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>401 Unauthorised</span> response and must be
              updated with the new value.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Passenger Traffic</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Passenger Traffic DMs</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Adjusts the die modifiers used by the Passenger Traffic tool when determining how many
            Low, Basic, Middle, and High passengers are seeking passage between two star systems.
            Each field is pre-filled with, and overrides, the corresponding sourcebook value; clear
            a field to fall back to the default.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Population ≤1 through 12</span> — applied for both the origin and destination world's population code, one DM per digit. Only ≤1, 6, 7 and 8+ are part of the original sourcebook design; the rest default to 0.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Starport A / B / C / D / E / X</span> — applied for both the origin and destination world's starport code. C and D are not part of the original sourcebook design and default to 0.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Amber Zone / Red Zone</span> — applied for both the origin and destination world's travel zone.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Rolling for High / Rolling for Low</span> — applied only to the qualifying roll for that passenger type.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Per Parsec Past First</span> — applied once for each parsec of distance beyond the first between the two systems.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Freight Traffic</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Freight Traffic DMs</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Adjusts the die modifiers used by the Freight Traffic tool when determining how many
            Incidental, Minor, and Major cargo lots are available for shipment between two star
            systems. Each field is pre-filled with, and overrides, the corresponding sourcebook
            value; clear a field to fall back to the default.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Population ≤1 through 12</span> — applied for both the origin and destination world's population code, one DM per digit. Only ≤1, 6, 7 and 8+ are part of the original sourcebook design; the rest default to 0.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Tech Level 0 through 16</span> — applied for both the origin and destination world's tech level digit, one DM per digit. Only ≤6 and 9+ are part of the original sourcebook design; the rest default to 0.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Starport A / B / C / D / E / X</span> — applied for both the origin and destination world's starport code. C and D are not part of the original sourcebook design and default to 0.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Amber Zone / Red Zone</span> — applied for both the origin and destination world's travel zone.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Major Cargo / Incidental Cargo</span> — applied only to the qualifying roll for that cargo lot type.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Per Parsec Past First</span> — applied once for each parsec of distance beyond the first between the two systems.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Mail</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Mail DMs</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Adjusts the die modifiers used by the Mail tool when determining whether mail is
            available for shipment between two star systems. Each field is pre-filled with, and
            overrides, the corresponding sourcebook value; clear a field to fall back to the
            default.
          </dd>
          <ul class='mt-2 space-y-2 list-disc list-inside'>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Freight Traffic DM -10 or less through 10 or more</span> — one DM per tier of the Freight Traffic DM computed for the same pair of worlds (the same total shown on the Freight tab).</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Ship is armed</span> — applied when the Travellers' ship is armed.</li>
            <li><span class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Origin world TL 5 or less</span> — applied when the origin world's tech level is 5 or less.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-4'>Speculative Trade</p>

      <dl class='space-y-6'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Local Broker Level</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            The campaign-wide default Broker skill level assumed for a hired local broker on the
            Commerce tool's Speculative Trade tab, when the <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Use
            Local Broker</span> option is checked. The sourcebook rolls this randomly as
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>2D/3</span> (2D6 divided by 3, rounded down); this
            setting fixes it at a single default instead, editable per-transaction on the tab itself.
            Defaults to <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>2</span>, the average result.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Local Broker Fee %</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            The campaign-wide default flat fee, as a percentage of the transaction price, charged
            by a hired local broker. Defaults to <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>10</span>,
            per the sourcebook.
          </dd>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class=' uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-highlight mb-3'>Bulk Operations</p>
      <dl class='space-y-4 text-sm'>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Regenerate All Sectors</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Enqueues generation jobs for every subsector in the campaign that has a build
            configuration assigned, regardless of whether the sector already contains star systems.
            Existing systems are replaced. Locked systems are always left untouched. The
            <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>Exclude visited subsectors</span> toggle next to
            the button is on by default; while on, any subsector containing a parsec the players
            have travelled to or from, per the jump log, is skipped entirely.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Populate Empty Sectors</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
            Enqueues generation jobs only for subsectors belonging to sectors that contain no star
            systems. Sectors that have already been generated are left untouched. The confirmation
            prompt displays the number of empty sectors and subsectors that will be affected.
          </dd>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class='pt-2 text-right uppercase font-mono  tracking-[0.22em] text-fg-muted'>
      REFERENCE 0417CS
    </div>

  </div>
<% end %>
